<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kediri Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1045 CE – 1222 CE · capital Daha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The East Javanese kingdom ruled from Daha, whose 12th-century court produced classics of Old Javanese poetry and grew rich on the spice trade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kediri, also called Panjalu, was the western half of the realm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a> divided around 1045, with its capital at Daha on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brantas-river">Brantas River</a>. For decades it is poorly documented, but by the 1130s it had absorbed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/janggala">Janggala</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayabaya">King Jayabaya</a> proclaimed 'Panjalu jayati', Panjalu is victorious.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kediri controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brantas-river">Brantas</a> valley and the ports that shipped Moluccan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> westward; Chinese writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-qufei">Zhou Qufei</a> counted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> among the richest lands overseas. Its court poets wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakawin-bharatayuddha">Kakawin Bharatayuddha</a>, completed in 1157 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayabaya">Jayabaya</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smaradahana">Smaradahana</a>. Jayabaya is also remembered in Javanese prophecy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kertajaya">Kertajaya</a>, quarrelled with the clergy, who sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ken-arok">Ken Arok</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/singhasari">Tumapel</a>. Ken Arok defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ganter">Ganter</a> in 1222 and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a>. Kediri revived briefly as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gelang-gelang">Gelang-gelang</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayakatwang">Jayakatwang</a> in 1292.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kediri-kingdom.jpg" alt="Kediri Kingdom" width="320" height="272"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southeast_Asia_trade_route_map_XIIcentury.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1045 CE – 1222 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Daha</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jayabaya, Kameshwara, Kertajaya</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism and Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Javanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Indonesia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Panjalu, Daha, Kadiri</dd></dl></aside></div>
